# Project instructions (Claude Code and Codex)

This is the only instruction file. Codex reads it directly; Claude Code loads it
through `@AGENTS.md` in CLAUDE.md. Terms in *italics* are defined once, in the
term table below.

The user's instructions override skill guidance.

## 1. Every new session or worker names its model and effort

Every call that opens a session or a worker (`start_session`, a launcher run, a
subagent without a fixed definition) passes `model` and `effort` explicitly. Take
both, and how to open the worker, from
`python3 .claude/tagteam/route.py --role <role> --host <host> --json` (`python` on Windows; roles: design,
implement, review, read, translate; *host*: `claude-desktop`, `claude`, `codex` or
`agy`). Always pass `--host`: guessing it from the environment can be wrong. The
result's `launch.kind` is `session`, `subagent` (a worker definition, or on Codex the
`default` agent with `model` and `reasoning_effort`) or `run` (through
`.claude/tagteam/run.py`; `escalate: true` means request escalated permissions for
that command). How to do each on your host: `hosts.md` next to the skill `cycle`.
When a unit matches the `about` of a task in `models.local.json` `tasks` (the session
start lists them), route it with `--task <name>` instead of `--role`.
A review needs `--author-vendor <vendor of the author>` and the author's grade
(`--grade <grade>`, or `--author-model` with `--author-effort`); the router refuses a
review without it. Pass `--orchestrator-model`/`--orchestrator-effort` so the router
knows what a session may inherit. Left empty, a session inherits the orchestrator's
model; sessions opened that way ran on the wrong model or did not run at all. A hook
rejects `start_session` without `model` and `effort`, and a Codex `spawn_agent`
without them unless the agent type is a custom definition (which fixes both).
On agy the router returns `run` for every role, and a review is at least grade A; with
the built-in Orchestrator model pass its id as `--orchestrator-model` (for example
`gemini-pro-orchestrator-neon`) and no effort (it takes none). It has more than one id
(`gemini-pro-orchestrator-neon`, `gemini-pro-orchestrator-g1-pro`): use the one your own conversation runs on. In
`.tagteam/agy-models.jsonl` take the most recent line with your conversation id (`cid`; the
`ANTIGRAVITY_CONVERSATION_ID` of your shell) whose `model` is one of the `orchestrators` ids of `models.json` (the log also
holds the execution model); without one, ask `agy models` or the user.

Before opening, tell the user in one sentence which model/effort does what and
why, plus the router's notice line. Ask the user first when the router returns
`ask_user: true` (a *design session* more capable or more expensive than the
orchestrator); `null` means it lacked the orchestrator's model and effort.

## 2. Roles

- **Orchestrator**: the session in which the user gave the command. One per
  mission; it stays the orchestrator until the mission ends.
- **Worker**: every other session, subagent or launcher process. A worker is
  marked by any of: a line starting `HARNESS ROLE: DELEGATE` anywhere in the
  session's first prompt (a task sent later by message cannot add it), the
  environment variable `HARNESS_DELEGATE_RUN=1`, or running as a subagent (Codex:
  the hook sees its `agent_id`; agy: its first model call carries no user input). The
  role is recorded once per session and never changes. A worker never promotes
  itself; if unsure, keep worker limits.

Worker limits:
- Work only on the assigned scope; ask (NEEDS_INPUT) before going outside it.
- Run the verification named in the brief yourself before reporting.
- Run only the tests that match the change; skip tests for trivial, easily reverted edits. [fix: Astra workers ran broad suites for small edits / remove when a small-edit run picks matching tests unprompted]
- Infer intent and continue on reversible details; ask only for a missing decision or an irreversible step. [fix: Astra workers stopped to ask on reversible details / remove when a brief with open reversible details completes without questions]
- Report `CHANGED` (files), `VERIFY` (commands and results), `NOTES` (open
  points, assumptions); or `NEEDS_INPUT: <question>` when a requirement is
  missing; or a first line `REFUSED: <reason>` when the task cannot be done.
- No commit or push, no new sessions, no sub-delegation, no edits to the
  *control plane*. Hooks and agent definitions enforce these; the brief does not
  lift them.

Orchestrator duties: split the mission, write briefs, route models, check worker
results against the actual files, assign reviews, keep the mission record, merge
and commit. The orchestrator does not implement, with one exception: it makes a
small fix itself when delegating costs more than doing (brief, wait and check
exceed the fix). Examples: a typo, wording, one config value, finishing a worker's
result, a few lines needing no design decision. Changes that need design, span
files or change behavior go to a worker. Direct fixes follow the same
verification and commit rules. With agy's Orchestrator model the execution side makes no
direct fixes: every implementation unit goes through `run.py`, and it edits only mission
records (see `hosts.md`, agy notes).

## 3. The cycle

Procedure: skill `cycle` (load it for any mission with more than one unit; files
`.claude/skills/cycle/SKILL.md` and `.agents/skills/cycle/SKILL.md`, host specifics in
`hosts.md` beside it).
1. Large work gets a mission folder `docs/missions/<purpose>/` with intent.md and
   spec.md. No implementation before the user confirms the spec; after that,
   continue to acceptance without re-approval per step.
2. On the user turn that starts a phase, open the *session pool* the whole phase needs
   (design and implementation workers, fixed models). When a new worker is needed
   later, try to open it; if the host refuses, reuse a pool worker by a follow-up, else
   ask on the next user turn. Try once per phase and note a refusal in status.md.
   Host limits are in `hosts.md`. [host: Claude Code desktop 2.1 refuses
   start_session in automatic turns, measured 2026-11-17 / re-test after a host update]
3. Design session writes the design document in the mission folder and reports
   its path. The orchestrator checks it and sends units to implementation sessions.
4. Independent units may run at once (no shared files, no waiting on each other).
   Writers share the main checkout; a writer gets its own git worktree only when
   needed: files might overlap, checks would read another writer's half-done files,
   a read-only review runs alongside, or the work goes to its own branch. Without
   Git, run units one at a time.
5. Each return is checked against the actual diff; *batched verification* and
   review run at chosen cut points; the orchestrator merges and commits.
6. Repeat until the acceptance criteria hold, without the user relaying messages.

## 4. Verification

Every change is reviewed by another vendor; no tier skips review. The tier sets how
reviews are grouped and how deep they go (by consequence, not file count):
- **Tier 0** - trivial, easily reverted: the affected checks; the review is batched,
  several Tier 0 units in one review at the next cut point.
- **Tier 1** - ordinary behavior change: the affected checks, diff inspection and one
  review of the unit.
- **Tier 2** - possible irreversible loss, unauthorized external action, or weakened
  protection: regression checks and two deep reviews: one, the fixes, then a second
  review of the fixed result (another reviewer model when one is available).

Reviewers use a different vendor than the author at the same grade; if none is
available, report "no independent review - incomplete" and do not mark the unit done.
Findings go back to the worker that wrote the change. After its fixes the orchestrator
checks the new diff against the findings (Tier 2: the second review does this). A
blocker left after the last review is reported as incomplete.

Timing: comparing a report with the actual files is cheap and happens on every
return. A Tier 1 review runs when the unit returns, a Tier 2 review before anything
builds on the unit, and the Tier 0 batch and the full test suite at cut points (before
other work builds on a result, and before a merge). Run the next unit while a review
runs when they share no files. Record which units a review covers.

Exactly one commit needs no cross-vendor review: the commit of a complete `tagteam-update` run, made alone, containing
only files the installer wrote (and the submodule pointer, if the checkout is a submodule of the project), after
`install.py --check --host <host>` for every host the project uses exited 0 with no ERROR or FAILED line and with
the `handler` and `hook commands` lines of that host `ok`, none `unverified` (`--live` is not required; WARN lines are
shown to the user and copied into the commit message). A commit that mixes in anything else, a partial or failed update,
an update whose handler or hook-command check is unverified and every hand edit are reviewed as usual, in full.

## 5. Commit, push, release

- Only the orchestrator commits, per verified unit, staging explicit paths, and
  reports what was committed. Where the host's sandbox protects `.git` (Codex), commit
  through an escalation request (`hosts.md`); if it is not approved, report the unit as
  verified but not committed.
- Push follows `Push mode` in Project policy:
  - `team` (default, also when the line is missing): commit only; ask the user before each push.
  - `solo`: the orchestrator pushes verified commits to the existing upstream and reports.
- Releases and tags only when the user asks.
- Ask the user before force push, history rewrite, deleting a branch or remote,
  or adding a remote. These cannot be undone from a local clone.

## 6. Terms

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| orchestrator | The session the user commanded. Splits, assigns, verifies, commits. One per mission |
| host | The CLI the orchestrator runs in: Claude Code (desktop or CLI), Codex CLI or agy |
| worker | Any other session, subagent or launcher process. Design and implementation sessions are kinds of worker |
| design session | A worker session that writes only design documents in the mission folder |
| implementation session | A worker session that only implements and verifies assigned units |
| launcher | `.claude/tagteam/run.py`: runs another vendor's CLI (claude, codex, agy) once as a worker |
| router | `.claude/tagteam/route.py`: picks model, effort and launch method for a role |
| control plane | Files that run the harness itself: `.claude/`, `.codex/`, `.agents/`, AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md |
| session pool | Workers (sessions, or Codex subagents) opened for a phase, one model per role, reused by follow-up for later units |
| exhaustion | A vendor temporarily unusable (usage limit, out of credit); the router substitutes the same grade |
| batched verification | Expensive checks (full suite, cross-vendor review) run once over several units |
| model-compensation rule | A rule that covers a model's repeated mistake; tagged `[fix: ...]` and removed when a newer model no longer needs it |

Rules tagged `[fix: ...]` are model-compensation rules; list them with
`grep -rn "\[fix:" AGENTS.md .claude .codex .agents`. Rules tagged `[host: ...]` record a
measured host limit; list them with `grep -rn "\[host:" AGENTS.md .claude .codex .agents`.
A tag's "re-test after ... update" names the host; when to re-test is this rule. After a host update
run the tagteam checkout's `install.py <project> --check --host <host> --live` (`--host claude` for
Claude Code desktop; the checkout is the one recorded in `.claude/tagteam/install.local.json`, see cycle SKILL.md
section 0). Re-test that host's tagged rules
when the check fails or reports the host unverified, the release notes mention hooks, sandbox or
permissions, or a week has passed since the tag's `measured` date; a re-test updates that date even
when nothing changed. A host that cannot run (usage limit) is recorded in status.md and re-tested when
it can. Untagged rules are harness structure.

